    "text_blocks": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "If your roof insurance claim is denied, you do not have to accept the decision as final\u2014many denials can be successfully challenged or reversed.",
        "snippet_highlighted_words": [
          "many denials can be successfully challenged or reversed"
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          0,
          1
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Immediate Steps to Take"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Read the Denial Letter: Carefully review the official written denial to see the exact reason cited (e.g., normal wear and tear, pre-existing damage, improper installation, or damage falling below your deductible).",
            "reference_indexes": [
              1,
              2,
              3
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Get an Independent Inspection: Hire a trusted, licensed local roofing contractor to inspect your roof. Ask them to look specifically for sudden storm or impact damage, take chalk marks and high-resolution photos, and write an independent report.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              4,
              5,
              6
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Collect Weather and Historical Evidence: Gather local storm reports, date-stamped satellite images (such as Google Earth), or certified hail/wind reports for the date of the loss. Collect past maintenance records and receipts.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              4,
              5,
              6,
              7
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Request a Re-inspection: Contact your insurance company to ask for a second inspection, and make sure your independent roofer is present when the new adjuster arrives so they can point out missed or misclassified damage.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              1,
              3,
              4
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "File a Formal Appeal: Submit a point-by-point written rebuttal letter with your contractor's report, photos, and weather data attached.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              3,
              5
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Escalation Options if the Appeal Fails"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Invoke the Policy Appraisal Clause: If you agree on the cause of damage but disagree on the cost, check your policy for an appraisal clause. You hire an independent appraiser, the insurance company hires one, and a neutral umpire resolves the difference.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              1,
              7
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Hire a Public Adjuster: Consider retaining a licensed public adjuster who works on your behalf (typically for a 10% to 15% fee of the claim payout) to re-evaluate and negotiate the damage.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              3,
              7
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "File a State Insurance Complaint: If you believe the insurer acted in bad faith or handled the claim unfairly, file a formal complaint with your state's department of insurance (for example, the Utah Insurance Department).",
            "reference_indexes": [
              6,
              7,
              8
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Consult an Attorney: For high-value claims or clear contract breaches, consult a dedicated insurance claim or property damage lawyer.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              5,
              9
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "To help narrow down your next move, tell me:"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "What specific reason did the insurance company list in your denial letter?"
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Did a local roofer inspect the damage yet?"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
